   "file": "04_cio_eu_manufacturer.md",
   "text": "# Interview 04 (fictional)\nInterviewee: Lukas W., CIO, industrial manufacturer headquartered in Germany, ~5,000 office staff across EU and US\nInterviewer: PM, Uplift\nDate: (mock)\n\nPM: What's your situation with AI tools?\nLukas: We run three: one vendor's assistant in the office suite, a coding agent for engineering, and a chat tool for everyone else. The board wants one number: is it paying off? I can't give it, because the usage data sits in three admin consoles with different definitions.\n\nPM: What would a useful view look like?\nLukas: Adoption and value by function and by workflow, across all tools, normalized. Plus license utilization, so I stop paying for seats nobody uses.\n\nPM: What constraints matter most?\nLukas: The works council must approve any system that could evaluate employee performance. Anything individual-level is a non-starter in Germany without their agreement. Data stays in the EU. Aggregation thresholds so you can't identify a person in a five-person team.\n\nPM: How long would approval take?\nLukas: If it's designed for privacy from day one, a few months. If not, never.\n\nPM: What would make you buy?\nLukas: Cross-tool visibility and a privacy design my works council signs off on quickly. Whoever solves that wins Europe.\n"
